  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1203 AM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nLittle change to the ongoing forecast, either in the actual\nnumbers or the reasoning behind them.\n\nDeep upper level trough over the western US and a upper ridge over\nthe Great Lakes will produce southwest flow aloft across the\nSouthern Plains through the end of the week and into the weekend.\nTemperatures will gradually climb into the low/mid 70s by Saturday\nafternoon. The upper trough will weaken and shift northeast, but\na shortwave will race across Oklahoma and Kansas Saturday and\nSaturday Night. Lift with this system stays north of the area so\nshould remain dry, but it will allow a cold front to push across \nWest Central Texas and drop temperatures about 10 degrees for\nSunday.\n\nA stronger negatively tilted trough will move across Texas for\nMonday into Tuesday. Moisture is limited but this system does look\nstrong enough to pull out some at least scattered showers across\nthe area. Rain chances are generally in the 30-50% range from the\nmodel blends and not going to argue much with that. Rainfall \ntotals look limited at around one tenth of an inch on average so \nimpacts will not be much. It will help reinforce the cooler air \nmass across the area and keep afternoon highs in the 50s. Right \nnow, forecast has lower 40s for lows in many areas, but if one \nnight happens to be a better radiational cooling night, than some \n30s will certainly be possible.",
